96
00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.790
as long as I take the reciprocal of this second fraction.

97
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.620
So what this is going to become then is 9/2,

98
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.477
instead of division, I do multiplication.

99
00:04:12.477 --> 00:04:15.300
And then I flip this upside down, I take its reciprocal.

100
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.970
So instead of 23/4, I get 4/23.

101
00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.190
And now this just turns into

102
00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.870
a multiplication problem, just like we had up here.

103
00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.640
And we know what to do with multiplication,

104
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.480
we multiply across our numerators,

105
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.793
nine times four is 36, two times 23 is 46.

106
00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.210
I can divide both the numerator

107
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.790
and the denominator by two, and I'll get 18/23.

108
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.900
And now this is a reduced fraction.

109
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.600
So I can leave my fraction that way.

110
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.260
It's not an improper fraction,

111
00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.220
so I'm not going to change it to a mixed number.

112
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.480
So my final answer then is 18/23.

113
00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.240
Let's look at one more division problem.

114
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.840
Again, I'll change these to improper fractions.

115
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.230
Four times two is eight,

116
00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.270
eight plus one is nine, so I get 9/4.

117
00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.950
Divided by, five times one is five, five plus one is six,

118
00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.770
so six over the original denominator of five.

119
00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.860
But this is going to become 9/4,

120
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.860
